    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the retirement and social security law, in relation to re-employment of retired persons by the board of higher education of the city of New York",
    "basePrintNo": "S7452",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7452-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Retirement and Social Security Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7452\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the retirement and social security law, in relation to\nre-employment of retired persons by the board of higher education of\nthe city of New York\n\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nThis bill would amend subparagraphs (3) and (5) of paragraph (a) of\nsubdivision 2 of § 211 of the Retirement and Social Security Law\n(\"RSSL\") to authorize the Board of Higher Education of the City of New\nYork (\"CUNY\"), in place of the City's Department of Citywide\nAdministrative Services (\"DCAS\"), to make the determination whether a\nretiree may be employed in positions of public service at CUNY without\na suspension or diminution of their retirement benefits.  These\nsubparagraphs are among the seven set forth in RSSL § 211(2)(a) that\ndelineate which officers or government entities are authorized to give\nsuch approval. Paragraph (3) currently designates the \"municipal civil\nservice commission of the city of New York\", which is now DCAS\n(formerly the City's Department of Personnel (\"DOP\")), as the body\nauthorized to determine whether a retiree can work in a classified\nservice position in either the Board of Education (now known as the\nNew York City Department of Education, or \"DOE\") or CUNY. Subparagraph\n(5) now provides that CUNY is the entity that grants such permission\nto persons seeking positions in the unclassified service of CUNY.\n\n REASONS FOR SUPPORT :\nSection 150 of the New York State Civil Service Law and § 1117 of the\nNew York City Charter provide, as a general rule, that a person\nretired from public service shall have his or her pension suspended\nduring any subsequent period of re-employment in public service.\nExceptions to this general rule, however, are provided by RSSL §§ 211\nand 212. Those sections permit re-employment in public service without\ndiminution or suspension of a retiree's pension in certain\nwell-defined instances and only after compliance with strict\nguidelines and procedures.\n\nOne such exception specified by RSSL § 211 provides that where a\nprospective public employer can make a compelling case for employing a\nretired person, such person can be re-employed with only a reduction\nof - as opposed to the suspension of - his or her pension or, in some\ncases, with no reduction at all. In accordance with RSSL § 211(2),\nrequests for these exemptions must be presented to one of seven\nofficers or entities in New York State.\n\nRSSL § 211(2)(a)(3) currently specifies that prospective re-employment\nwith the City of New York or the classified services (that is,\nnon-pedagogical staffs) of either DOE or CUNY must be reviewed by\nDCAS, the City's municipal civil service commission. With respect to\nCity employment and employment in the non-pedagogical staff of DOE,\nDCAS's responsibility to review such requests is appropriate. As the\nmunicipal commission for these entities, DCAS administers the civil\nservice system for them, including classifying their civil service\ntitles, administering civil service examinations for competitive class\ntitle, and certifying the names of eligible candidates to fill\nvacancies in positions. Moreover, until 1988, this responsibility was\nappropriate with respect to reemployment with CUNY, because, until\n1988, the City's DOP also served as the municipal civil service\ncommission for CUNY. In 1988, however, the Board of Trustees of CUNY\nwas authorized to establish its own civil service commission, separate\nand independent from DOP. The Board appointed the first Commission in\n1989, and adopted the Rules and Regulations of the Civil Service\nCommission on July 18,1989. CUNY's creation of its own independent\nmunicipal civil service commission meant that it was no longer under\nthe jurisdiction of DOP. Despite this significant change, however, the\nlanguage in RSSL § 211 was not similarly changed; consequently, DCAS\nstill approves approximately two new waivers and, on the average,\nthree § 211 waiver renewals for CUNY each year. This bill would change\nthe law as it is currently written to exclude CUNY from the list of\nentities for which DCAS will consider requests for § 211 waivers.\n\nBecause this review for re-employment in public service still must be\nconducted by some entity, this bill also contains a corresponding\nproposed amendment to subparagraph (5) of RSSL § 211(2)(a), which\nwould give CUNY the authority to evaluate waivers for retired persons\nseeking re-employment in its own classified service, in addition to\nthe waivers it already evaluates for re-employment in its unclassified\nservice. Such change reflects the proposed amendment to subparagraph\n(3) above, and vests in CUNY the authority to review all waiver\nrequests for its own prospective employees.\n\nIn view of the fact that CUNY is no longer under the jurisdiction of\nDCAS for the administration of its civil service, it no longer makes\nlegal or operational sense to require that DCAS evaluate CUNY requests\nfor retired persons to return to its service without a suspension or\ndiminution of existing pension benefits.\n\nAccordingly, the Mayor urges the earliest possible favorable\nconsideration of this proposal by the Legislature.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
