    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S7452A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act to amend the retirement and social security law, in relation to\nre-employment of retired persons by the board of higher education of the\ncity of New York\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nThis bill would amend subparagraphs (3) and (5) of paragraph (a) of\nsubdivision 2 of § 211 of the Retirement and Social Security Law\n(\"RSSL\") to authorize the Board of Higher Education of the City of New\nYork (\"CUNY\"), in place of the City's Department of Citywide Administra-\ntive Services (\"DCAS\"), to make the determination whether a retiree may\nbe employed in positions of public service at CUNY without a suspension\nor diminution of their retirement benefits.  These subparagraphs are\namong the seven set forth in RSSL § 211(2) (a) that delineate which\nofficers or government entities are authorized to give such approval.\nParagraph (3) currently designates the \"municipal civil service commis-\nsion of the city of New York\", which is now DCAS (formerly the City's\nDepartment of Personnel (\"DOP\")), as the body authorized to determine\nwhether a retiree can work in a classified service position in either\nthe Board of Education (now known as the New York City Department of\nEducation, or \"DOE\") or CUNY. Subparagraph (5) now provides that CUNY is\nthe entity that grants such permission to persons seeking positions in\nthe unclassified service of CUNY.\n\nREASONS FOR SUPPORT:\nSection 150 of the New York State Civil Service Law and § 1117 of the\nNew York City Charter provide, as a general rule, that a person retired\nfrom public service shall have his or her pension suspended during any\nsubsequent period of re-employment in public service.  Exceptions to\nthis general rule, however, are provided by RSSL §§ 211 and 212. Those\nsections permit re-employment in public service without diminution or\nsuspension of a retiree's pension in certain well-defined instances and\nonly after compliance with strict guidelines and procedures.\n\nOne such exception specified by RSSL § 211 provides that where a\nprospective public employer can make a compelling case for employing a\nretired person, such person can be re-employed with only a reduction of\n- as opposed to the suspension of - his or her pension or, in some\ncases, with no reduction at all. In accordance with RSSL § 211(2),\nrequests for these exemptions must be presented to one of seven officers\nor entities in New York State.\n\nRSSL § 211(2) (a) (3) currently specifies that prospective re-employment\nwith the City of New York or the classified services (that is, non-peda-\ngogical staffs) of either DOE or CUNY must be reviewed by DCAS, the\nCity's municipal civil service commission. With respect to City employ-\nment and employment in the non-pedagogical staff of DOE, DCAS's respon-\nsibility to review such requests is appropriate. As the municipal\ncommission for these entities, DCAS administers the civil service system\n\nfor them, including classifying their civil service titles, administer-\ning civil service examinations for competitive class title, and certify-\ning the names of eligible candidates to fill vacancies in positions.\nMoreover, until 1988, this responsibility was appropriate with respect\nto reemployment with CUNY, because, until 1988, the City's DOP also\nserved as the municipal civil service commission for CUNY. In 1988,\nhowever, the Board of Trustees of CUNY was authorized to establish its\nown civil service commission, separate and independent from DOP.  The\nBoard appointed the first Commission in 1989, and adopted the Rules and\nRegulations of the Civil Service Commission on July 18, 1989. CUNY's\ncreation of its own independent municipal civil service commission meant\nthat it was no longer under the jurisdiction of DOP. Despite this\nsignificant change, however, the language in RSSL § 211 was not similar-\nly changed; consequently, DCAS still approves approximately two new\nwaivers and, on the average, three § 211 waiver renewals for CUNY each\nyear. This bill would change the law as it is currently written to\nexclude CUNY from the list of entities for which DCAS will consider\nrequests for § 211 waivers.\n\nBecause this review for re-employment in public service still must be\nconducted by some entity, this bill also contains a corresponding\nproposed amendment to subparagraph (5) of RSSL § 211 (2) (a), which\nwould give CUNY the authority to evaluate waivers for retired persons\nseeking re-employment in its own classified service, in addition to the\nwaivers it already evaluates for re-employment in its unclassified\nservice. Such change reflects the proposed amendment to subparagraph (3)\nabove, and vests in CUNY the authority to review all waiver requests for\nits own prospective employees.\n\nIn view of the fact that CUNY is no longer under the jurisdiction of\nDCAS for the administration of its civil service, it no longer makes\nlegal or operational sense to require that DCAS evaluate CUNY requests\nfor retired persons to return to its service without a suspension or\ndiminution of existing pension benefits.\n\nAccordingly, the Mayor urges the earliest possible favorable consider-\nation of this proposal by the Legislature.\n\nFINANCIAL IMPACT:\nMinimal, to address any possible administrative costs associated with\nchanging the approving entity for these 211 waivers.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n\n",
